 ## Overview
-Chunked Volume Rendering is a memory-efficient inference technique that integrates color and density along rays by processing sample points in fixed-size batches (chunks) rather than all at once [S1, S3, unofficial]. It solves the GPU memory bottleneck associated with large ray bundles or high-resolution sampling by trading off memory usage for computational throughput, allowing for trade-offs between time and memory [S3, unofficial]. It differs from its parent [Inference Ray Marching and Integration](2148-inference-ray-marching-and-integration.md) by explicitly managing memory via chunking, and from [Sparse Ray Marching (Training)](3890-sparse-ray-marching-training.md) by focusing on inference-time memory constraints rather than training-time sparsity optimization.
+Chunked Volume Rendering is a memory-efficient inference technique that integrates color and density along rays by processing sampled points in fixed-size batches (chunks) [S1, S3, unofficial]. It solves the GPU memory bottleneck associated with rendering large scenes or high-resolution implicit fields by allowing a trade-off between memory usage and computational throughput [S3, unofficial]. It differs from its parent [Inference Ray Marching and Integration](2148-inference-ray-marching-and-integration.md) by explicitly batching the forward pass to constrain VRAM, and from its sibling [Inference Ray Compositing](3790-inference-ray-compositing.md) by focusing on the chunked accumulation of radiance rather than just the compositing logic.
 
