 This method accelerates volume rendering by combining chunked ray batching with an occupancy grid estimator to skip empty space. It solves the performance bottleneck of uniform ray marching in sparse or large scenes by only sampling regions where the radiance field has non-zero density. It differs from its parent [Chunked Volume Rendering](4310-chunked-volume-rendering.md) by integrating a specific hierarchical occupancy grid for adaptive sampling, and from its sibling [Block-based Volume Rendering Strategy](13140-block-based-volume-rendering-strategy.md) by using a continuous grid-based estimator rather than discrete block structures.
 
+### [Partitioned Volume Rendering Derivation](10384-partitioned-volume-rendering-derivation.md)
+Partitioned Volume Rendering Derivation provides the mathematical foundation for distributing volumetric rendering across multiple disjoint spatial segments (partitions) along a ray. It solves the problem of correctly accumulating color, transmittance, and auxiliary losses (such as distortion) when a ray traverses multiple independent volume tiles, which may be processed by different compute units. It sets itself apart from standard single-volume ray marching by explicitly decomposing global integrals into sums of local segment integrals weighted by upstream transmittance, enabling parallel prefix-scan accumulation.
+
 ### [Block-based Volume Rendering Strategy](13140-block-based-volume-rendering-strategy.md)
 A rendering strategy for large scenes partitioned into blocks, where each block uses a separate shader due to web texture unit limits. It solves the problem where standard volume rendering fails because sampling points across a ray may belong to different blocks loaded by different shaders, breaking 3D consistency and correct occlusion. It sets itself apart from its siblings by enforcing sequential rendering and post-hoc accumulation rather than attempting to load all resources into a single pass or using occupancy grids to skip empty space.
 
-### Tricubic B-Spline Reconstruction
-An opt-in technique to fix texel staircase artifacts in chunked rendering, though it does not remove ringing [S5, unofficial]. It requires a 2-voxel halo around chunks. Implementation involves gating cubic interpolation per draw on halo size $\ge 2$ and ensuring the volume is not a label volume (including coarse-floor cubes) [S5, unofficial]. The public default halo remains $[1,1,1]$, and enabling this feature triggers re-planning of loaded volumes when the halo setter fires [S5, unofficial].
-
-### Chunk 0 Eviction Fix
-A fix for WebGL2 volume blanking caused by `draw()` early-returning on null `volumeTexture`/`volumeGradientTexture` when chunk 0 was evicted [S5, unofficial]. Since these textures are best-effort aliases of chunk 0 in chunked volumes, evicting chunk 0 killed the whole volume. The fix removes the early-return on null textures, refreshes aliases after `manager.remap()` destroys unmatched chunks, and prevents the single-volume path from deleting textures owned by the chunked cache entry during switching [S5, unofficial].
-
-### 4-in-1 Material Workaround
-A workaround for lighting artifacts in chunked rendering by hacking the Material of the Heterogeneous Volume Actor (HVA) to sample from four different chunks as if they were one [S6, unofficial]. It uses a Sparse Volume Texture (SVT) of full resolution spanning the volume with values only at min/max corners to trick the HVA, feeding its internal lighting cache with values from all chunks [S6, unofficial]. This results in a heavy performance hit (~1fps), compensated by allowing interactive navigation with fewer samples (e.g., 32) and rendering with more samples (e.g., 512) for final views [S6, unofficial]. Console command `r.HeterogeneousVolumes.MaxStepCount=32` controls interactivity sampling [S6, unofficial].
-
-### Partitioned Volume Rendering Derivation
-A mathematical derivation for distributed NeRF rendering where accumulated color and distortion loss are decomposed per segment (partition) [S7].
-*   **Accumulated Color:** $C(t_1 \rightarrow t_{N+1}) = \sum_{k=1}^N T(t_1 \rightarrow t_k) C(t_k \rightarrow t_{k+1})$, where $C(t_k \rightarrow t_{k+1}) = \int_{t_k}^{t_{k+1}} T(t_k \rightarrow t) \sigma(t) c(t) dt$ [S7].
-*   **Distortion Loss:** $\mathcal{L}_{dist}(t_1 \rightarrow t_{N+1}) = 2 \sum_{k=1}^N T(t_1 \rightarrow t_k) S(t_1 \rightarrow t_k) + \sum_{k=1}^N T(t_1 \rightarrow t_k)^2 \mathcal{L}_{dist}(t_k \rightarrow t_{k+1})$ [S7].
-*   **Intermediate Term:** $S(t_1 \rightarrow t_k) = D(t_k \rightarrow t_{k+1})A(t_1 \rightarrow t_k) - A(t_k \rightarrow t_{k+1})D(t_1 \rightarrow t_k)$, where $A$ is accumulated weight and $D$ is accumulated depth [S7].
-This enables per-segment color accumulation and distributed training across multiple GPUs [S7].
-
-### Surface Volume Rendering
-A variant that renders at the estimated intersection point of the ray with the surface, rather than integrating along the entire ray [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Intersection Depth:** $\text{n\_depth} = \frac{\sum_t \text{sampled\_depth}_t \times \text{n\_probs}_t}{\sum_t \text{n\_probs}_t}$ [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Boundary Depth:** $\text{n\_bound} = \text{sampled\_depth} + \frac{\text{sampled\_distance}}{2}$ [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Rendered Color:** $\text{color} = \text{texture} \times (1 - \text{missed})$ [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Eikonal Term:** $\text{eikonal} = (\|\text{normal}\|_2 - 1)^2$ [S3, unofficial].
-It involves computing normalized probabilities, determining the voxel index at intersection, and gathering texture/normal at that depth [S3, unofficial].
