 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is reduced from $O(N \cdot B)$ to $O(\text{chunk\_size} \cdot B)$, where $N$ is total samples and $B$ is batch size [S3, unofficial]. Time complexity is linear with respect to the number of samples, but overhead increases slightly due to chunk management and concatenation [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** In Unreal Engine 5, chunked rendering with sparse volume textures achieved ~10-20 fps for large datasets, whereas non-chunked approaches suffered from VRAM fragmentation and API limits [S2, unofficial]. The "4-in-1" workaround for seamless lighting dropped framerates to ~1fps [S6, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Allows rendering of datasets larger than available VRAM by streaming chunks [S2, S5, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables rendering of large scenes on hardware with limited VRAM; supports early stopping for further speedup [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Chunk boundaries can introduce visual artifacts (incorrect lighting) if not handled carefully (e.g., via the 4-in-1 workaround or occupancy grids) [S2, S6, unofficial]. Requires careful tuning of `chunk_size` to balance memory and performance [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot B)$, where $N$ is the number of samples per ray and $B$ is the number of rays. Chunking does not change the asymptotic complexity but increases overhead due to loop iterations.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(B_{chunk} \cdot N)$, where $B_{chunk}$ is the `chunk_size`. This allows rendering scenes with $B \gg B_{chunk}$ by trading memory for time [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** In Unreal Engine, chunking allows rendering datasets >1 gigavoxel, but introduces visual artifacts at chunk borders if lighting is computed independently per chunk [S2, unofficial]. A workaround using a "4-in-1" material can achieve seamless lighting but drops framerates to ~1fps [S6, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Enables rendering of very large scenes or high-resolution implicit fields on GPUs with limited VRAM [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Chunking can introduce artifacts if not handled correctly (e.g., lighting discontinuities) [S2, unofficial]. Early stopping requires careful tolerance tuning to avoid premature termination [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Chunked volume rendering originated as a solution to memory constraints in neural volume rendering (NeRF) and traditional volume rendering. It is closely associated with the implementation of memory-efficient ray marching in frameworks like PyTorch3D and Fairseq's neural rendering extensions [S1, S3, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in real-time rendering engines (like Unreal Engine) to handle large medical or geological datasets that exceed texture unit limits or VRAM capacities [S2, S6, unofficial]. The "NeRF-style" terminology reflects its adoption in neural radiance field inference pipelines [S1, S3, unofficial].
+Chunked Volume Rendering is based on the volume rendering equations defined in [Mildenhall et al. 2020] (NeRF) [S1, unofficial]. It has been implemented in various contexts, including the TBRayMarcher plugin for Unreal Engine [S2, unofficial], the NSVF framework by Facebook Research [S3, unofficial], and web-based viewers like Niivue [S5, unofficial]. The technique is essential for real-time inference of large neural radiance fields and volumetric datasets.
 
